<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qutb Shahi dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1518–<a href="/years/1687">1687</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Qutb Shahis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qutb Shahi dynasty ruled the Golconda Sultanate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 1518 to 1687. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quli-qutb-mulk">Sultan Quli</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, these Shia rulers grew rich on diamonds and textiles, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in 1591 and patronised Telugu, Persian and Dakhni literature. The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> in 1687 after a long siege, ending the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Qutb Shahi dynasty" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LG02 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qutbshahi_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
